  "description": "Heritage list-reporter aliases execute through the identical Core code path (issue #669, slice H4): the three reporter aliases `bf`/`bl`/`se` are 'alternate spellings only — no new semantics' (spec/conformance.md#heritage), so this program's full event stream is byte-identical (payloads included) to the same program written with the Core reporter names `butfirst`/`butlast`/`sentence` — the command-alias proof `heritage-aliases-execute-like-core` extended to REPORTERS, which appear in expression position (as arguments to `print`, composed as `bf bl :l`, and as an assignment RHS `make \"s\" se …`) rather than as leading statements. Unlike the command aliases (normalized at the statement dispatch chokepoint), a reporter alias is normalized to its `canonical` at the single EXPRESSION chokepoint (`resolveHeritageAliasName`, shared by `evaluateCall` and `isSupportedExpression`) before any evaluator predicate runs, so every `print` payload carries the reporter's reported value and the `procedure-enter`/`procedure-exit` names carry the canonical Core name — no alias spelling reaches any payload. The aliases are exercised in awkward positions the reader must still canonicalize: composed with one another (line 4), inside a `repeat [ … ]` body (line 6), inside a procedure body (`tail`, renamed from `rest` by issue #839 -- `rest` is a Sound primitive and so a built-in name; the replacement is the same length, so every span is unchanged), and as an assignment RHS (line 11). The reader records `canonical` unconditionally, and so does this run: no `executeOptions` is set, so it uses `execute()`'s default claimed set (`RUN_PROFILES` = every profile this implementation supports), and the events below are identical for every value of the `profiles` array above — measured through the harness's `produce()`, including with that array empty. That array is NOT the run's claimed set: `spec/execution-model.md:673-680` governs the latter, which a fixture names through `executeOptions.profiles`, a different field (`scripts/harness/index.mjs:655-665`). What this array does is select which DAG subsets the fixture runs in and carry its under-declaration claim. `heritage` must not be trimmed from it because `profileGateErrors` (issue #790) rejects the array without it; `data` need not be listed because that gate first expands the declared set through `closureOf`, and `spec/conformance.md:305` normatively puts Data in any Heritage claim — measured, `[\"core-language\", \"heritage\"]` passes the gate while `[\"core-language\", \"data\"]` is rejected. Note that `se` does not put it there: `se` aliases the CORE reporter `sentence` (`spec/built-in-names.json`), which is why `spec/conformance.md:305` lists it among the aliases that \"alias Core names and add no edge\". See issue #1156 for the superseded rationale.",
